# Run genrt on QEMU virt

## Requirements and contents

Install QEMU providing `qemu-system-aarch64` on a host with a POSIX `/bin/sh`.
Use a QEMU version supporting `virt`, GICv2, `cortex-a72`, the generic loader,
and `-action reboot=reset,shutdown=poweroff`. The build's QEMU version is
recorded in `manifest.json`. No Rust toolchain, repository, xtask, device-tree
tools, or network access is required to run this extracted bundle.

The archive contains:

- `run.sh`: executable launcher.
- `genrt-aarch64.elf`: the production kernel shared by all CPU topologies.
- `initramfs.cpio`: production userspace, including `/init` and `/bin` programs.
- `initramfs.manifest.json`: initramfs entry metadata and hashes.
- @DTB_LIST@: topology-specific device trees.
- `manifest.json`: version, build tools, features, and payload SHA-256 hashes.
- `LICENSE`: MIT license; `RUN.md`: this guide.

## Quick start

From the extracted directory:

```sh
./run.sh
./run.sh -c @MAX_CPUS@
./run.sh --help
```

Run one command at a time. The default is one CPU; `-c N` and `--cpus N`
accept integers 1 through @MAX_CPUS@. The launcher automatically selects the
matching DTB and `-smp` count. It can be invoked by path from another working
directory, including when the extraction directory contains spaces. It uses
only packaged artifacts and does not generate DTBs at runtime.

Only the listed options are supported; duplicate CPU options, positional
arguments, and arbitrary QEMU flags are rejected. CPU values must use the
listed decimal form (no sign or leading zeros).

## Direct QEMU invocation

Without the launcher, change to the extracted directory and run:

```sh
@DEFAULT_COMMAND@
```

For @MAX_CPUS@ CPUs, the equivalent command is:

```sh
@SMP_COMMAND@
```

Always change both `-smp` and the DTB filename together for other supported
counts. Do not change loader addresses or add `cpu-num` to the data loaders.

## Console and power control

Boot enters the genrt userspace shell. UART is attached to this terminal with
`-serial stdio`; display, monitor, and networking are disabled.
Use guest `reboot` to reset and boot again in the same QEMU process, or
`poweroff` to shut down QEMU. Shell `exit` only terminates `/init`, not QEMU.

This serial configuration has no multiplexed monitor escape: Ctrl-A X is not
a quit command. Use Ctrl-C (QEMU's stdio signal handling) to terminate QEMU,
or send a termination signal from another host terminal. The launcher uses
`exec`, so QEMU receives signals directly and its exit status is preserved.
See the [QEMU invocation reference](https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/system/qemu-manpage.html)
for stdio signal and character-device multiplexing details.

## Troubleshooting

- Missing QEMU: install a package providing `qemu-system-aarch64` and ensure
  it is in `PATH`. The launcher reports this with exit status 127.
- Invalid CPU count or malformed option: use `./run.sh --help`; validation
  fails before QEMU starts, with exit status 2.
- Missing artifact: re-extract the complete archive and keep `run.sh` beside
  its kernel, initramfs, and DTBs. Do not copy the launcher alone.
- Unsupported machine, CPU, or `-action`: use a QEMU version supporting the
  configuration above, preferably the version recorded in `manifest.json`.
